1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Artificial Intelligence\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 15 Jan 2026 (this version), latest version 20 Apr 2026 (cite5†v2 )]\nL9: # Title:Evidence-Augmented Policy Optimization with Reward Co-Evolution for Long-Context Reasoning\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Xin Guan , cite7†Zijian Li , cite8†Shen Huang , cite9†Pengjun Xie , cite10†Jingren Zhou , cite11†Jiuxin Cao L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Evidence-Augmented Policy Optimization with Reward Co-Evolution for Long-Context Reasoning, by Xin Guan and 5 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite12†View PDF cite13†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:While Reinforcement Learning (RL) has advanced LLM reasoning, applying it to long-context scenarios is hindered by sparsity of outcome rewards. This limitation fails to penalize ungrounded \"lucky guesses,\" leaving the critical process of needle-in-a-haystack evidence retrieval largely unsupervised. To address this, we propose EAPO (Evidence-Augmented Policy Optimization).\nL17: We first establish the Evidence-Augmented Reasoning paradigm, validating via Tree-Structured Evidence Sampling that precise evidence extraction is the decisive bottleneck for long-context reasoning. Guided by this insight, EAPO introduces a specialized RL algorithm where a reward model computes a Group-Relative Evidence Reward, providing dense process supervision to explicitly improve evidence quality.\nL18: To sustain accurate supervision throughout training, we further incorporate an Adaptive Reward-Policy Co-Evolution mechanism. This mechanism iteratively refines the reward model using outcome-consistent rollouts, sharpening its discriminative capability to ensure precise process guidance. Comprehensive evaluations across eight benchmarks demonstrate that EAPO significantly enhances long-context reasoning performance compared to SOTA baselines.\nL19: Subjects:  | Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite14†arXiv:2601.10306 [cs.AI]\nL21:    | (or cite15†arXiv:2601.10306v1 [cs.AI] for this version)\nL22:    | cite16†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2601.10306†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Xin Guan [cite17†view email ]\nL26: [v1] Thu, 15 Jan 2026 11:40:57 UTC (702 KB)\nL27: cite5†[v2] Mon, 20 Apr 2026 12:01:33 UTC (1,006 KB)\nL28: \nL29: Full-text links:\nL30: \nL31: ## Access Paper:\nL32: \nL33: View a PDF of the paper titled Evidence-Augmented Policy Optimization with Reward Co-Evolution for Long-Context Reasoning, by Xin Guan and 5 other authors\nL34: \nL35:   * cite12†View PDF L36:   * cite13†HTML (experimental) L37:   * cite18†TeX Source L38: \nL39: cite19†view license L40: ### Current browse context:\nL41: \nL42: cs.AI\nL43: \nL44: cite20†< prev |   cite21†next > L45: \nL46: cite22†new | cite23†recent | cite24†2026-01 L47: \nL48: Change to browse by:\nL49: \nL50: cite25†cs L51: cite26†cs.CL L52: \nL53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * cite27†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L56:   * cite28†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L57:   * cite29†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L58: \nL59: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL60: \nL61: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL62: \nL63: [Button: ×]\nL64: \nL65: loading...\nL66: \nL67: Data provided by:\nL68: \nL69: ### Bookmark\nL70: \nL71: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL72: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL73: \nL74: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL75: \nL76: Bibliographic Explorer (cite30†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL77: \nL78: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL79: \nL80: Connected Papers (cite31†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com )\nL81: \nL82: [Input] Litmaps Toggle\nL83: \nL84: Litmaps (